```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: Long-horizon tool-calling agent teams under KV-memory pressure whose traces contain delayed-callback tool-result spans (cold at eviction, hot later) and who can operate at moderate KV budgets (≥40% of full context) with a realizable handle-recurrence signal achieving critical-handle AUC ≳0.64. The win is specific to retaining the task-critical callback chain, not to improving average token recall.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark-only at L0. The simulation engineers the cold-then-hot structure and shows CAUSAL beats H2O only on the task-chain metric (M2) at moderate-to-large budgets, while tying H2O on aggregate availability (M1) in 23/25 cells and tying at tight budgets (frac≤0.3) or crit_AUC≤0.588. The effect decays as AUC drops and leaves large oracle headroom (oracle 1.0 vs CAUSAL 0.3–0.55). No evidence yet that production agent traces violate persistence-of-importance or that a paged kernel + H2O would not already be sufficient.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [Requires L1 validation that real tool-agent traces exhibit delayed cold callbacks (persistence-of-importance violation) — currently unproven; Needs runtime span-boundary detection with ~10% error tolerance and extraction of decayed past-attention plus n-gram/handle features from a live model; Predictor must sustain critical-handle AUC ≥0.64 in production noise, while H2O matches CAUSAL on M1 with zero added complexity; Benefit vanishes at tight budgets (≤30%) where KV pressure is most acute; Integration cost vs deployed H2O/SnapKV (simple accumulated-attention eviction) with no M1 gain; Large headroom to oracle indicates the noisy proxy captures only a fraction of the theoretical win]
RATIONALE: Packet reports PARTIAL-HELD: “On aggregate availability (M1) it merely ties H2O” and “beats H2O in only 2/25 cells, both at top AUC”; “ties at tight budgets (frac<=0.3) and at crit-AUC<=0.588; LARGE oracle headroom (oracle 1.0 where CAUSAL 0.3-0.55)”; “The win is ENGINEERED INTO the generative model (we built delayed cold callbacks H2O can't catch)… It does NOT show real tool traces have this structure — that is the single biggest open risk”; “On the task-chain metric, H2O and RT score 0.000 essentially EVERYWHERE — they NEVER retain the delayed cold callback handles. CAUSAL is the only realizable policy that does”; “At crit_AUC=0.588 it ties at 0 (both fail). So the win needs crit_AUC ≳ 0.64 AND a non-tight budget”; “A real-model L1 (real attention as predictor on real tool-agent traces, real task pass@1) is the natural ask if candidate-grade.” These bounds confine the win to a narrow, unvalidated regime and make the negative result (use H2O for M1; CAUSAL only for M2 with budget headroom) the actionable product guidance.
```
